.TH PCBFP 5
.SH NAME
pcbfp \- footprint of a part
.SH DESCRIPTION
A footprint file, named for the footprint, gives its pads and its
courtyard in mm from its origin, as seen from the top, a record a
line; # starts a comment.
.PP
.RS
.nf
pad NAME rect|rrect|circle X Y W H [nopaste]
hole NAME X Y D DRILL [keep K]
court X0 Y0 X1 Y1
silk W X Y X Y ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pad is copper on the side of its part, soldered through the opening
of its paste; a pad without paste (nopaste) is a contact pad, never
soldered (a spring's or a block's ground contact), which a via may
stand in (pcb(5), rule viagap).
A hole is plated, of diameter
D on every layer, drilled DRILL.
A hole a screw goes through says
.B keep
K, the diameter of its head or washer: a circle of diameter K round
the hole keeps the courtyards of the parts of both sides off, and the
vias, whatever their nets; a hole without keep keeps nothing but its
copper clear.
The pads of one name are one pin and join inside the part.
The courtyard keeps the parts of its side off: no two courtyards on
one side may overlap.
A silk line, of width W, goes on the silkscreen of the part's side.
Footprints are found in the directory footprints next to the board
source, then in the libraries.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# r0603 -- 0603 chip resistor
pad 1 rrect -0.825 0 0.8 0.95
pad 2 rrect 0.825 0 0.8 0.95
court -1.48 -0.73 1.48 0.73
.fi
.PP
A body outline with a mark at pin 1:
.PP
.nf
silk 0.12 -3.4 2.55 1.95 2.55
silk 0.12 -1.95 -2.55 1.95 -2.55
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5)
